                <pb facs="0889" n="769" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg769"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/3840: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-26T12:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 26,
                                1922—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received May 26—6:51
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>233. My 230 May 23, 8 p.m. My British, French, and Japanese colleagues have
                    agreed that if they are approached by the Chinese Government regarding loan they
                    will adopt the attitude that the proposal cannot be entertained in the present
                    state of affairs; that when, however, a government has been formed which, in
                    their opinion, affords a prospect of stability and is able to give satisfactory
                    assurances for unification they will be prepared to recommend some scheme for
                    financial assistance to the consideration of their Governments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
